@MindWanderer

&nbsp;

I feel like that most of your concerns about the system are concerns that are about any new system - If a GM is not experienced in the system, there is lots of room for error. I am not sure that I can muster up much worry/concern over a lot of them -&nbsp;

[quote="MindWanderer"]

My opinions haven&#39;t really changed, although they&#39;ve become more refined in some ways.

* Most of my concerns were about the adventure modules in the starter kit, not the core rules.&nbsp; Obviously, nothing has changed my mind about those.

*Challenges that are set up as one or more Overcome checks that need to be made really need to be spelled out, or else players won&#39;t bite on them.&nbsp; Ones that are required need to be hand-fed to the players (e.g. written on index cards right off the bat).&nbsp; Optional ones can be hinted at or &quot;gated&quot; behind other events or attempts, but the GM needs to assume that they won&#39;t go anywhere, and balance challenges appropriately.

*Challenges are still pretty railroad-y.&nbsp; The GM decides what needs to be done, the players figure out some way to justify their Powers and Qualities (and ideally a Principle) to accomplish it.&nbsp; Sometimes that can result in some cool storytelling, but other times it was really strained.


[/quote]


&nbsp;

 All of these concerns, while completely valid, are about how experienced a GM/Player/group is. The more experienced the group with freeform RPG style (FATE comes to mind, and there are a couple of other systems I can&#39;t recall the name of off the top of my head but are far less constrained rules wise, than say DnD) the less these concerns become a issue, in my view. I feel like that is an inherent thing about -any- &#39;less than mechanically rigid&#39; system, and if you really want to get the most out of Roleplaying its a concern about -every- system.&nbsp; 

 You can have skill checks in DnD,WoD,FATE, which the players need to figure out there is something to do, just like these Overcome Checks. - Certainly in a game of DnD you could not haver any skill checks and just have combat, but then its not a RPG its a tactical battle game. If players walk into a bar and need to find the hidden entrance into the sewers, thats a skill check. Its also an Overcome check in this sytem. If players are in a battle and their spells aren&#39;t working to hit the wizard, but the scene description said he had a big crystal on a pedastal behind him - thats a skill check to figure out how to get past his shield. Or an Overcome in this system. 

 Your concerns that the players may not know to do this or bite on it are valid - but they are valid for every game. If players are not going to think to act outside of &#39;Me Kronk, Me Hit&#39; then any system is going to have to GM&#39;d differently.&nbsp; 

 &nbsp;  
[quote="MindWanderer"]


*If you want to design an effective character, the single most important thing is to choose two Principles that let you use your Max die in the widest possible range of circumstances.&nbsp; Ones that require specific circumstances (Cosmic), that are hard to justify (Time Traveler, at least when you&#39;re from the past), or that don&#39;t let you use your Max die (Speed) make it so that you&#39;ll rack up a lot more failures and twists, and miss opportunities for Hero Points.&nbsp; Choosing your other abilities wisely helps (especially mass attacks and persistent boosts/hinders), but the Principles are critical.


[/quote]


&nbsp;

I can completely agree and yet disagree with these. If you are looking to make the most effective character that can handle the situation given in the quickest and most effective way possible, yes. You are completely right. WIthout maximizing the use of your and your groups Principles youare missing out on a lot of bonuses and more effective rolls than a standard Risky Action or Overcome check.

This I disagree with out of principle (heh) as the type of RPGer I am. I am -not- looking to be most effective in an RPG. I&#39;m looking to tell a story, and a lot of a good story will be NOT being effective. This is clearly the type of RPGers that Christopher and Adam are - you can see it in the gleam with the Twists in Christophers eyes and why Adam almost -always- takes a twist if there is an option. Because its far more fun

In this sense, I feel a lot of the abilities are super powerful (Completely destroy X minions? wow. That can clear a scene - or some of the Boost Hinder combos? Yeah jeeze those are cool) and that relates to the &#39;We&#39;re SUPER HEROES&#39; - but the Twist system is completely a storytelling mechanic to make the game interesting.

 Also, I get the feeling from the way the sheets are designed that Principles are supposed to be the most important thing to your character. They are your &#39;Class&#39; or your &#39;Clan and Bloodline&#39; - they are what defines your character. 

&nbsp;

 &nbsp;  
[quote="MindWanderer"]


*Christopher made a big point out of playing by the rules (except for the mecha battle and the cheer boosts), but he did exploit the vagueness of the rules extensively.&nbsp;

* The twists he came up with were frequently much more or much less powerful than the recommended ones in the book.&nbsp; A GM that&#39;s just a little off in their judgment could make adventures much harder or much easier than intended. 

&nbsp;


[/quote]


&nbsp;

These are back to my first point - Experienced groups/players are going to always be better at RPGs in any system than inexperienced. This system i feel is far more friendly to inexperienced because of the lack of rules, but thats a personal opinion. I can see where you&#39;re coming from here.

&nbsp;

I am curious though on how you feel he exploited the vaugness of the rules? In a bad way? (Was it confusing to the players? DId it make the game less fun?) or in a good way (to make some things roll smother, to make some things change direction if needed?)


[quote="MindWanderer"]


*Regarding initiative: the players got fatigued.&nbsp; Early on, they tried to choose their turn order very carefully to avoid negative consequences.&nbsp; Later, they started making their choices almost at random.&nbsp; They completely gave up on using abilities that lasted until their next turn, and never bothered setting up combos with boosts and hinders.&nbsp; Christopher stopped exploiting every mistake they made, too, probably because he didn&#39;t want to punish them for it anymore (like he did in the first couple of games).&nbsp; So I don&#39;t feel like the system ended up working well.


[/quote]


&nbsp;

 I&#39;m interested in these points - I agree, that after a while they stopped worrying/carring about some things. I see it as some slightly different reasons. They realized that in most situations, worrying about the Badguys all going twice in a row isn&#39;t actually that big a deal - Sure they get to actions, but as a result the Heroes will get two turns in a row too. It balances out and really isn&#39;t that big a deal, so they only thought about it in extereme situations (The final boss fight) 

&nbsp;

 As for exploiting ever mistake - thats not something I believe any GM should do.&nbsp; They should exploit mistakes that make the game interesting, but if players are just being human and not their acutal SuperHuman characters, then they shouldn&#39;t be punished for that. I am not a Super Detective with a logical brain and an understanding of&nbsp; connections like Sherlock Holmes/Batman/Wraith but my character might be and I shouldn&#39;t be punished because I am not smart enough to actually be that. We aren&#39;t playing ourselves, were playing Characters we want to experience, and good GM&#39;s don&#39;t exploit that difference, they help craft a story that shows that thing we want to be in the limelight. 

&nbsp;


[quote="MindWanderer"]


* The system is really straightforward, but it&#39;s still fiddly for inexperienced players.&nbsp; Maggie and Rae forgot about their inherent abilities and reactions nearly every time they would have been relevant.&nbsp; Rae took basically the whole game to understand the difference between abilities and powers/qualities.&nbsp; They all invoked their hero points and collected trades only rarely.&nbsp; Having taught D&amp;D to new players, especially 4th edition, I feel like there&#39;s a gap here--D&amp;D is overall a much more complicated game, but it&#39;s less error-prone to get started with.


[/quote]


&nbsp;

This is another Agree/Disagree point for me - I agree, it seems like Rae had some difficulty, and never really used her abilities on her sheet - I think that isn&#39;t so much a problem with the system, as the situation. A brand new, never before played an RPG before with 2 very experienced RPGers (and one somewhat, I think Maggie has played RPGs before) is now doing so on camera before a crowd. Thats some pressure! Its hard enough for me to Roleplay out what an NPC or my Character does in front of my closest friends, trying to remember to be my character, which if you arent a naturally extroverted type can be very hard to do, and to learn a brand new system, and do that all under the watchful eyes/ears of hundreds of people?&nbsp; While there are two other people in the room who have done this sort of thing for decades and are all over the system as well? Wow. I would forget a lot of stuff too.

So I don&#39;t know how good of a meter stick this is for teaching the system.&nbsp;


[quote="MindWanderer"]


*The Scene Tracker is indeed iffy sometimes.&nbsp; The villain being able to accelerate it is particularly problematic--when that happens, the players run a real risk of losing due to it.&nbsp; A villain that really &quot;wants to win&quot; will be better off accelerating the scene tracker than trying to damage the heroes.&nbsp; Of course, the system is designed such that the GM really can&#39;t go all-out trying to &quot;beat&quot; the players, otherwise they&#39;ll just have a bunch of minions gang up on one hero.

*The game isn&#39;t about &quot;progression.&quot;&nbsp; It just isn&#39;t, get over it.&nbsp; On the plus side, that means it&#39;s well-suited for shorter series and one-shots.&nbsp; I think people who want to play campaigns of 20+ sessions will start feeling bored with their characters, but then, the superhero genre really isn&#39;t about that.&nbsp; I think Critical Hits could design a spinoff system in a fantasy/sci-fi setting that used the core Sentinels system and build a new game with leveled progression, but that would be something else entirely.

[/quote]

&nbsp;

Completely valid concerns. Completely. And you also made your own responses that I would make - thats just this system. Its not for everything, and thats fine. It does what its good at and thats what its going to do.&nbsp;

Also, as a Villain is controlled by the GM, its my opinion the GM is there to facilitate the story, not to &#39;Win&#39;. Win for the GM is making the players feel something, making them feel like having tears for the loss of an NPC, or rage at an villain, or fear at a situation, or horror at a concept. Not&nbsp; &quot;Beat them all into the ground&quot; - so a villain won&#39;t necessarily do whats &#39;best&#39; for his/her concept, they will do whats best to make a compelling story.

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

All of your concerns I can see the validity of, MindWanderer. They make sense. They also, in my opinion, stem from a completely different view of RPGs th an mine - you strike me as someone who far more enjoys the combat of DnD than the roleplaying - though you probably enjoy the RP as well, but when the swords come out and the magic starts getting flung about you might be someone who starts to have more fun there.

Nothing wrong with that, nothing at all. I myself am the more fluid type of player who would be just as keen to never get in a fight. Note, I am also an unashamed minmaxing Twink. I can, and have, made characters in every system I play in that can one shot baddies and completely destroy, mechanically, anything they come across - there is merit to this, which I think is why I see a lot of merit in a lot of your concerns. (This Twinkyness comes because some of my original rp group was very twinky, and in order to feel like i wasn&#39;t useless, i had to learn to be the same) My natural inclination, and when I have the most fun in table top situations with friends, is not to be that Twink, however. Its to have lots of weaknesses, and take twists and flaws and play them up to the max. Get into situations where the GM can really put the screws on us RP wise, if not mechanically, and put us in situations where we face these twists.

&nbsp;

As a GM, i am -very- much a social/rp/twist GM. I love to run plots where combat is almost non existant. Where the issues are all cerebral in nature. In that feeling, this sytem is amazing. It will make what combat there is go pretty fast, and still create lots of amazing roleplaying situations - I point to Rae&#39;s moves against the big bad in the final episode - those were amazing oppertunities to tell a story! Even if she didn&#39;t use anything on her sheet other than Lunar Orb, she told a hell of an awesome story with it!

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

So, in conclusion, I hear your concerns (not that I can do anything about them, but I am listening!) And i feel like I understand them, even if on a basic fundamnetal level my view goes a completely opposite direction. Thank you for voicing them!

&nbsp;

Thanks for putting in your comments/concerns/thoughts Jay Green! I haven&#39;t seen you around here before (not surprising, because I am pretty sparse on the forums these days) but welcome and thanks for participating!

[quote="Jay Green"]

Thoughts and notes after watching the character creation session and Issue #1.&nbsp; I will follow up with more as I go.Character creation uses random die rolls.&nbsp; I am kind of curious what the purpose of this is.&nbsp; It seemed a little bit tacked on, maybe to satisfy people who just want random dice rolls because reasons.&nbsp; Maybe useful for generating ideas if you really just don&#39;t know.&nbsp; Maybe someone on the design team has some deeper insights here though.&nbsp; I know games I like have seemingly weird stuff in character creation that actually works nicely in practice (Name lists in dungeon world, for example).&nbsp;&nbsp;Character creation might be a little bit lenghty.&nbsp; It might be something that can be done individually outside of an actual play session though, at least in part, which can mitigate this.&nbsp; This is not a concern, just an observation.&nbsp;

&nbsp;

[/quote]

&nbsp;

I agree here - i am not a fan of random character generation ever. I have a concept I want to play when I start gaming, and always want to make it. However, that being said, I think that they said in the character creation video that the system is optional and just for helping you get ideas if you don&#39;t have any. (of course, its an RPG. every system is optional)


[quote="Jay Green"]


&nbsp;

A lot of what you get when listening to a charismatic storyteller like Christopher is going to have nothing to do with the game itself, and everything to do with the person running the game.&nbsp; So it requires some careful attention to separate out the two parts of this, and without the rules in front of me to reference, I still can&#39;t fully differentiate between what is the GM and what is the rules.&nbsp; So everything has to be taken with a grain of salt.At 26 minutes in to the first issue, Christopher asks Craig to make an overcome roll to investigate an energy signature he detects.&nbsp; Its not entirely clear what the point of having him role here is.&nbsp; Is there a specific rule that says when a roll is triggered, or is it just GM discretion?&nbsp; Is the GM just using this as a generic &quot;investigate roll&quot;, with all the problems that come with that?&nbsp; What would have happened if he had failed the investigation roll?&nbsp; My guess is that things would have worked out almost identically, because saying &quot;You find nothing, I guess we stop playing&quot; doesn&#39;t work for good story telling.&nbsp; As it stands, his &quot;success&quot; did not seem very valuable.&nbsp; All he got was &quot;Thats sure some energy signature there.&nbsp; Lots of magic&quot;.&nbsp; Which isn&#39;t useful for him beyond saying &quot;Go talk to your teammate&quot; which is a given regardless of the investigation result.&nbsp; Even if he hadn&#39;t been given the detail that it was magical, one of the other 3 player would have been given that detail, since they had even more ability to understand magic than he did.&nbsp;&nbsp;Another overcome role is made at the 54 minute mark.&nbsp; This one the GM rules that one of the players principles can&#39;t be used, but when the player fails, the &quot;major twist&quot; is just that the previously overruled principle kicks in and gives the information anyways.&nbsp; Again, what are the actual rules on overcomes and investigations?&nbsp; Do the rules explicitly tell GMs not to do these time wasting behaviors, and the GM here just didn&#39;t care, or do they not give any meaningful guidance at all?&nbsp; Do the rules actually encourage this self defeating behavior?&nbsp; This one isn&#39;t quite as bad as the first one I mentioned.&nbsp; Its actually possible for the adventure to continue here without successful knowledge checks.This next one is less of a complaint about the game, and more a complaint about the way designers and fans have inaccurately described the game.&nbsp; There are multiple points where mechanical decisions are made before the fiction is consulted, sometimes without even bothering to check the fiction.&nbsp; Two examples:&nbsp; Starblight gets chomped by a dragon and the GM informs the player that his next turn can only be an overcome action.&nbsp; Later, Violet Heart uses a rage ribbons attack against some minions.&nbsp; The attack misses, meaning the minions are not destroyed but they are reduced a die size.&nbsp; No one ever says what this means in the fiction though.&nbsp; This is probably fine.&nbsp; It might be tedious, in this particular system, to always go back to the fiction.&nbsp; But the fact that this is even possible shows that the game is fundamentally incompatible with PbtA design principles.&nbsp; So its frustrating that so many people, including the actual designers of the game, have claimed Apocalypse World is a source for the design, when it absolutely is not, at least in the presented materials we have.

[/quote]

&nbsp;

Well, I think others already addressed these - but they do feel like GM issues rather than system issues to me, but as you said without the Rules in front of you its hard to seperate the two!

&nbsp;

Thank you for adding your thoughts to this discussion!
